@noindent
Each subsystem has a single responsibility and communicates only through
plain Python dicts.  No subsystem imports from the CLI layer.


@node The Agent Loop
@section The Agent Loop
@cindex agent loop

This is the full sequence of events for @command{codebud run "your request"}:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{CLI} parses the arguments and constructs an @code{AgentCore}.
@code{AgentCore.__init__} creates all subsystems in dependency order:
@code{Sandbox} → @code{ToolRegistry} → @code{SafetyEngine} →
@code{MemoryStore} → @code{LLMPlanner} → @code{Executor}.

@item
@strong{CLI} starts the @code{ProgressIndicator} thread, then calls
@code{AgentCore.handle_user_message(message, on_chunk=...)}.

@item
@strong{AgentCore} searches the project index, if the project has one
(@code{codebud.rag.context.retrieve()}), and delegates to
@code{LLMPlanner.generate_plan(message, history, context_hits)}.

@item
@strong{LLMPlanner} calls @code{_build_prompt()}, which embeds the tool
catalogue (from @code{ToolRegistry.describe_tools()}) and safety rules
(from @code{SafetyEngine.describe_rules()}) into a structured few-shot
prompt, followed by the conversation history and then the retrieved
project context, right before the request (so everything above it stays
the same between turns and Ollama's prompt cache covers it).

@item
@strong{LLMPlanner} calls @code{call_llm(prompt, on_chunk=...)} via the
LLM stub.  Tokens stream back from Ollama; each fragment is forwarded to
@code{on_chunk}, which updates the progress display.

@item
@strong{LLMPlanner} strips any Markdown code fences from the raw output
and attempts @code{json.loads()}.  If parsing fails, it retries once
with a minimal JSON-only prompt.  If the plan parses but a step misses an
argument its tool requires, it retries once with the same prompt plus a
note of what was wrong, then returns @code{plan_invalid}.  If parsing
fails again, it returns
@code{@{"status": "plan_error", ...@}}, and @code{AgentCore} asks the model
for a plain conversational answer instead (@code{call_llm_plain()}, given
the same project context and asked to cite files from it), returned as
@code{@{"status": "chat_reply", "reply": ...@}}.  A plan rejected by the
agent mode or the safety policy comes back as @code{plan_refused}, and an
invalid one as @code{plan_invalid}; both are reported as such.

@item
@strong{LLMPlanner} validates the parsed JSON:
@code{_validate_plan_structure()} checks that every step has @code{id},
@code{description}, @code{tool}, and @code{args}; that the named tool
exists in the registry; and that @code{SafetyEngine.validate_step(step)}
passes.

@item
@strong{AgentCore} re-validates the returned plan with
@code{_validate_plan()} (same checks, defence-in-depth).

@item
@strong{CLI} receives the validated plan dict and prints it, then runs
the steps one at a time with @code{Executor.execute_plan()}.
@command{run} asks before each powerful command and web search unless
@option{--yes} is given (every step with @option{-i};
@code{AgentCore.confirmation_reason(step)} says which steps and why).  @command{chat} first asks
@samp{Run this plan? [y/N/r(evise)]}, then prompts per step depending on
the agent mode (@pxref{Agent Modes}).

@item
@strong{Executor} iterates the step list.  For each step it first asks
@code{SafetyEngine.may_execute(step)} (refusing with @code{step_refused}),
then calls @code{ToolRegistry.get(tool_name).run(args)}.  For @code{command} steps
this delegates to @code{Sandbox.run_command(cmd)}.

@item
@strong{Executor} calls @code{MemoryStore.add_snapshot(step, result)}
after each completed step.  The snapshot is persisted to
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/memory/entries.jsonl} immediately.

@item
@strong{Executor} stops and returns a @code{step_error} dict if any step
returns a non-zero @code{returncode} or raises an exception.

@item
@strong{CLI} prints each step's outcome: full stdout in @command{run} and
@command{plan -x}; in @command{chat} a 15-line preview (full with
@option{--verbose}, or afterwards with @code{/output}).
@end enumerate


@node AgentCore
@section AgentCore
@cindex AgentCore

@code{AgentCore} is the entry point for all agent work.  The CLI creates
one instance and calls @code{handle_user_message()} once per turn.  In
@command{chat} mode the same instance is reused across turns so that
@code{MemoryStore} accumulates a session history.

@strong{Public API:}

@table @code
@item handle_user_message(message, on_chunk=None)
Generate a plan and return it.  Never executes steps.
Returns @code{@{"status": "ok", "plan": [...]@}},
@code{@{"status": "chat_reply", "reply": "..."@}} when no valid plan could
be made, @code{@{"status": "plan_refused", "error": "..."@}} when the agent
mode or the safety policy forbids a step (the error names it and says
why), @code{@{"status": "plan_invalid", "error": "..."@}} when steps still
miss required tool arguments after one retry, or
@code{@{"status": "plan_error", ...@}} for a malformed plan.
@item get_history(), clear_history(), undo_last_turn(), restore_history(history)
Read and change the conversation history (@code{/history}, @code{/clear},
@code{/undo}, @option{--resume}).
@item compact_history(keep_last=2, on_chunk=None), context_usage()
@code{/compact}, and the estimate behind the 70% context warning.
@item get_rag(), set_rag(on), last_context
Whether requests get project context from the index (@option{--no-rag},
@code{/rag on|off}), and the chunks the last request got (@code{/sources}).
@item get_mode(), set_mode(mode), is_powerful(step)
The agent mode and whether a step needs confirmation.
@item get_model(), set_model(model, on_chunk=None)
The active model; switching validates and warms it.
@item close()
Stop the model heartbeat.
@end table

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/core.py}


@node LLMPlanner
@section LLMPlanner
@cindex LLMPlanner
@cindex prompt, construction
@cindex retry logic

@code{LLMPlanner} turns a user message into a validated plan dict.

@subsection Prompt construction

@code{_build_prompt()} trims the conversation history to fit the context
window and @code{_render_prompt()} assembles the prompt from these
sections, followed by the agent-mode note (edit mode only), the history and
the request:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Critical rules} --- seven numbered instructions telling the model
to output only JSON and to pick the right tool for each operation (e.g.
``use @code{command} not @code{file_read} to list directories'').

@item
@strong{Available tools} --- the output of @code{ToolRegistry.describe_tools()},
which lists each tool name, its description, and its @code{usage_hint}.

@item
@strong{Safety rules} --- the output of @code{SafetyEngine.describe_rules()},
which lists the harmless and powerful commands and the trusted sequences.

@item
@strong{Response format} --- the exact JSON schema expected.

@item
@strong{Few-shot examples} --- five worked examples covering: list files,
run tests, write a file, a multi-step read-and-patch task, and a
@code{plan_error} for unsupported requests.
@end enumerate

@subsection Retry logic

@code{generate_plan()} makes at most two LLM calls:

@enumerate
@item
First call with the full prompt.  If @code{json.loads()} succeeds and the
plan validates, it is returned immediately.  If parsing fails, @code{None}
is returned from @code{_attempt()} to signal a retry.

@item
Retry with @code{_build_retry_prompt()}: a minimal two-paragraph prompt
that says only ``output valid JSON'' and lists the tool names.  Most
models that returned prose on the first try respond correctly here.

@item
If both attempts fail, @code{plan_error: "LLM returned invalid JSON"} is
returned.
@end enumerate

An empty LLM response short-circuits the retry: it returns
@code{plan_error: "LLM returned empty output"} immediately without a
second call.

The prompt includes up to the last six conversation turns.  Before sending,
@code{_build_prompt()} checks the estimated size against the context window
(@env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX} minus a 1,024-token reserve for the answer) and drops
the oldest turns until it fits, so the instructions at the start of the
prompt are never truncated by Ollama.  When nothing needs dropping the
prompt is byte-for-byte unchanged, which keeps Ollama's cached prompt
prefix valid between turns.

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/planner.py}


@node SafetyEngine
@section SafetyEngine
@cindex SafetyEngine
@cindex safety validation

@code{SafetyEngine} is consulted at three points: during plan validation
inside @code{LLMPlanner._validate_plan_structure()}, again inside
@code{AgentCore._validate_plan()}, and by @code{Executor} through
@code{may_execute()} right before each step runs.

For @code{command} steps, @code{validate_step(step)} splits the command
into words as the sandbox does and requires them to begin with an entry in
@file{harmless_commands.json} or @file{powerful_commands.json} (whole
words, not string prefixes); some arguments are refused regardless
(@code{find -exec}, @code{python -c}, @dots{}; @pxref{The Safety Engine}).
A rejected step fails the whole plan, which comes back as
@code{plan_refused} naming the step and the reason
(@code{SafetyEngine.refusal_reason(step)}).

All other tool types (@code{file_write}, @code{file_read}, @code{patch},
@code{web_search}, @code{search_embeddings}) are accepted
unconditionally at the @code{SafetyEngine} level; the Sandbox provides a
second layer of protection for shell commands only.

@subheading Agent modes
@cindex modes
@cindex edit mode
@cindex plan mode

The engine also enforces the active @dfn{mode} (@file{src/codebud/modes.py}):

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .88
@headitem Mode @tab Effect
@item @code{ask} @tab Default.  No extra restriction.
@item @code{auto} @tab No extra restriction (chat skips the per-step prompts
except for powerful commands).
@item @code{full-auto} @tab No extra restriction (chat asks nothing; the
allow-list and refused arguments still apply).
@item @code{plan} @tab Plans may be made, but @code{Executor} refuses to run any step.
@item @code{edit} @tab Only @code{file_read}, @code{file_write} and @code{patch}
steps are valid; every @code{command} step is rejected.
@end multitable

Restrictions are enforced twice: when a plan is validated, and again by
@code{Executor} right before each step runs (@code{may_execute()}), so a
plan made in one mode cannot run after switching to a stricter one.  A
plan rejected only because of the mode comes back as @code{plan_refused}
(``The plan uses tools not allowed in edit mode: command.'') rather than a
generic error, and is not turned into a chat reply.  Only @code{edit} mode
adds a line to the planner prompt; in the other modes the prompt is
unchanged, so Ollama's cached prefix stays valid.

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/safety.py}


@node Executor
@section Executor
@cindex Executor
@cindex step execution

@code{Executor.execute_plan(plan_output)} walks the @code{plan} array and
executes each step in sequence.

For each step:

@enumerate
@item Ask @code{SafetyEngine.may_execute(step)}; if the mode or policy
forbids the step, return @code{@{"status": "step_refused"@}} without running it.
@item Look up the tool instance via @code{ToolRegistry.get(tool_name)}.
@item Call @code{tool.run(args)}; if it raises, return
@code{@{"status": "step_error"@}}.
@item Validate the result dict has @code{stdout}, @code{stderr}, @code{returncode}.
@item Append a snapshot to @code{MemoryStore}.
@item If @code{returncode != 0}, return @code{@{"status": "step_error"@}}.
@end enumerate

The executor returns @code{@{"status": "ok", "results": @{step_id: result, ...@}@}}
when all steps succeed.

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/executor.py}


@node Sandbox
@section Sandbox
@cindex Sandbox
@cindex subprocess
@cindex timeout

@code{Sandbox.run_command(cmd)} is the only place in Codebud where a
subprocess is launched.  It provides a lightweight second safety layer
independent of the JSON policy files.

@table @strong
@item Catastrophic commands
Powering off, erasing a disk, and recursively deleting a vital directory
(@file{/}, a system directory, a home directory) are refused whatever the
policy allows, judged on the parsed command
(@code{safety.catastrophic_reason()}, @pxref{The Sandbox}).  The sandbox
checks again before the command is passed to @code{subprocess.run()}.

@item Timeout
Every command has a 10-second wall-clock timeout enforced by
@code{subprocess.run(timeout=...)}.  A timed-out command returns
@code{returncode=1} and a timeout message in @code{stderr}.

@item No shell expansion
Commands are split with @code{shlex.split()} and passed as a list, so
shell metacharacters (@code{&&}, @code{|}, @code{>}) are not interpreted.
@end table

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/sandbox.py}


@node MemoryStore
@section MemoryStore
@cindex MemoryStore
@cindex memory timeline
@cindex entries.jsonl

@code{MemoryStore} maintains a chronological list of execution snapshots.
Each snapshot contains:

@example
@{
  "timestamp": "2026-04-23T15:30:00.000000+00:00",
  "data": @{
    "step":   @{ "id": "step_0", "description": "...",
                "tool": "command", "args": @{...@} @},
    "result": @{ "stdout": "...", "stderr": "", "returncode": 0,
                "metadata": @{...@} @}
  @}
@}
@end example

Snapshots are stored as JSON Lines in @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/memory/entries.jsonl}:
one snapshot per line, appended (and @code{fsync}ed) after every completed
step.  Earlier lines are never rewritten, so a crash mid-plan cannot lose
earlier results.

@itemize @bullet
@item
Two codebud processes may record at the same time: appends are
serialised with an exclusive @code{flock} on
@file{~/.local/share/codebud/memory/entries.jsonl.lock}.

@item
A line that cannot be parsed (for example, a write cut off by a crash) is
skipped with a warning on standard error and left in the file untouched.

@item
A legacy @file{entries.json} next to it (a single JSON array, used before
JSON Lines) is converted automatically on first start and kept as
@file{entries.json.migrated}.  If it cannot be parsed it is renamed to
@file{entries.json.corrupt-@var{timestamp}} instead, and a new history is
started.
@end itemize

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/memory.py}


@node Sessions
@section Sessions
@cindex sessions
@cindex checkpoints
@cindex sessions directory

@file{src/codebud/sessions.py} saves each @command{chat} conversation so it can be
resumed later.  Where @code{MemoryStore} records executed @emph{steps},
a session records the @emph{conversation}: the history the planner sees,
plus the active model.  Each session is one JSON file:

@example
$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/sessions/20261025-093012-7f3a.json
@{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "20261025-093012-7f3a",
  "name": "refactor-memory",
  "created": "2026-10-25T09:30:12+00:00",
  "updated": "2026-10-25T09:41:57+00:00",
  "model": "qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M",
  "mode": "ask",
  "history": [ @{"role": "user", "content": "..."@}, ... ]
@}
@end example

@code{name} is @code{null} until the session is named with @code{/save}.
Several chats may run at the same time, so:

@itemize @bullet
@item
The ID is a UTC timestamp plus a random four-hex-digit suffix, and the file
is reserved with an exclusive create (@code{O_EXCL}); on a collision a new
suffix is drawn.  Two chats never share an ID, even within the same second.

@item
A chat holds an exclusive @code{flock} on @file{@var{id}.lock} while the
session is open.  Opening a session that another chat has open fails with
@code{SessionInUseError}.

@item
Every save replaces the file atomically (temporary file, @code{fsync},
rename), so a crash never leaves a half-written session.

@item
The format is versioned; a file written by a newer Codebud is refused
rather than misread.  A corrupt file is reported and skipped when listing.
@end itemize

@command{chat} creates the session lazily, the first time there is
something to save, and saves again whenever the history or the agent mode
changes (a turn, @code{/undo}, @code{/compact}, @code{/clear}, @code{/mode})
and on exit.

@noindent
Source: @file{src/codebud/sessions.py}, @file{src/codebud/fsutil.py}


@node ToolRegistry
@section ToolRegistry
@cindex ToolRegistry

@code{ToolRegistry} holds the seven built-in tool instances and provides
three methods:

@table @code
@item get(name)
Return the tool instance.

@item has(name)
Return @code{True} if the tool exists.

@item describe_tools()
Return a multi-line string for the planner prompt: one line per tool with
